###
post: Moving integrals from Cartesian to spherical functions
agent: claude

`lib/hf/cart2sph` builds `M`, the block-diagonal matrix whose column `m`
holds the unit-Cartesian coefficients of spherical function `m`. The integral
kernels produce everything in unit-Cartesian functions; this module moves the
results into the spherical basis.

A one-electron matrix has one function on each side, so it picks up one `M`
on each side:

    X_sph[p, q] = sum_ab M[a, p] X[a, b] M[b, q],     X_sph = M^T X M

An electron repulsion integral `(ij|kl)` has four functions, so it picks up
four:

    G_sph[p, q, r, s] = sum_ijkl M[i, p] M[j, q] M[k, r] M[l, s] G[i, j, k, l]

Done as written, that last sum costs `n^8`. Done one index at a time (sum
over `l` first, keep the result, then `k`, then `j`, then `i`) it costs four
passes of `n^5`. Those are the quarter transformations of `tr4_quarter`.

**Two forms each.** The dense forms (`tr2_dense`, `tr4_quarter`) treat `M` as
a full matrix and never look at the shell structure. The per-block forms
(`tr2_blocks`, `tr4_blocks`) use the fact that `M` is zero between shells: a
spherical function of shell `a` is built only from Cartesian components of
shell `a`, so the spherical block of shells `(a, b)` comes from the Cartesian
block of the same shells and nothing else. For the integrals, each shell
quartet is read out of the packed vector, transformed with four small `M_l`
blocks, and written back into the packed spherical vector. The per-block
forms do far less arithmetic; the dense forms are simpler to trust. They share
no loops, so agreement between them checks both.

All four take the transform-stage signature `(X: Arr, M: Arr, shells: Arr)
-> Arr`, so a registry of them runs through
`sweep3_arr`. The ERI forms take and return packed-8 vectors. The two
`tr2_back_*` forms go the other way, `M D M^T`: a spherical density becomes
the Cartesian one whose contraction with Cartesian integrals gives the same
energy, which is what a Cartesian J/K build needs.

The tests hold the transforms to PySCF's own spherical integrals for water in
6-31G*, cc-pVDZ and def2-SVP, within 1e-13.

Used with `import hf.transform`, after `lib/hf/basis`, `lib/hf/eri_store`
and `lib/hf/cart2sph`.
###

from hf.basis import bas_nao_cart, bas_nao_sph, SH_CART0, SH_L, SH_NCART, SH_NSPH, SH_SPH0
from hf.eri_store import es_index8, es_len8, es_pack8, es_pair, es_unpack8
import hf.cart2sph
from hf.md_eri import mderi_screened

# the fuzz test's seeds, 1 to 8
TR4_FUZZ_NSEED = 8
# tr4_rand_shells: 3 to 5 shells, each s to f (4 values of l), and a shell
# that would take the Cartesian count past 15 becomes an s
TR4_RAND_NSH_MIN = 3
TR4_RAND_NSH_SPREAD = 3.0
TR4_RAND_NL = 4.0
TR4_RAND_NCART_MAX = 15

# ---- one-electron matrices ----------------------------------------------------

#| `M^T X M` as two full matrix products, `[nao_sph, nao_sph]`. The shells
#| pack is used only for the two sizes.
def tr2_dense(X: Arr, M: Arr, shells: Arr) -> Arr {
    return transpose(M) @ (X @ M)
}

#| `M^T X M` one shell pair at a time: the (a, b) spherical block is
#| `M_a^T X_ab M_b`, where `M_a` is shell a's diagonal block of M. Every pair
#| is done, so X need not be symmetric.
#@ 21711c66
def tr2_blocks(X: Arr, M: Arr, shells: Arr) -> Arr {
    ns = bas_nao_sph(shells)
    Y = zeros(ns, ns)
    for a to len(shells) {
        ca = round(shells[a, SH_CART0])
        sa = round(shells[a, SH_SPH0])
        na = round(shells[a, SH_NCART])
        ma = round(shells[a, SH_NSPH])
        for b to len(shells) {
            cb = round(shells[b, SH_CART0])
            sb = round(shells[b, SH_SPH0])
            nb = round(shells[b, SH_NCART])
            mb = round(shells[b, SH_NSPH])
            # X_ab M_b, then M_a^T times that
            H = X[ca:ca + na, cb:cb + nb] @ M[cb:cb + nb, sb:sb + mb]
            Y[sa:sa + ma, sb:sb + mb] = transpose(M[ca:ca + na, sa:sa + ma]) @ H
        }
    }
    return Y
}

#| `M D M^T`, `[nao_cart, nao_cart]`: a spherical density as the Cartesian
#| one with the same contractions, since Tr(D M^T X M) = Tr((M D M^T) X).
def tr2_back_dense(D: Arr, M: Arr, shells: Arr) -> Arr {
    return M @ D @ transpose(M)
}

#| `M D M^T` one shell pair at a time.
def tr2_back_blocks(D: Arr, M: Arr, shells: Arr) -> Arr {
    nc = bas_nao_cart(shells)
    Y = zeros(nc, nc)
    for a to len(shells) {
        ca = round(shells[a, SH_CART0])
        sa = round(shells[a, SH_SPH0])
        na = round(shells[a, SH_NCART])
        ma = round(shells[a, SH_NSPH])
        for b to len(shells) {
            cb = round(shells[b, SH_CART0])
            sb = round(shells[b, SH_SPH0])
            nb = round(shells[b, SH_NCART])
            mb = round(shells[b, SH_NSPH])
            H = M[ca:ca + na, sa:sa + ma] @ D[sa:sa + ma, sb:sb + mb]
            Y[ca:ca + na, cb:cb + nb] = H @ transpose(M[cb:cb + nb, sb:sb + mb])
        }
    }
    return Y
}

# ---- electron repulsion integrals ---------------------------------------------

#| The dense four-index transform of a dense `[nc, nc, nc, nc]` tensor, one
#| index per pass: l, then k, then j, then i. Returns `[ns, ns, ns, ns]`.
def tr4_dense(G: Arr, M: Arr, nc, ns) -> Arr {
    #: one index per pass: nc^4 ns work each, not nc^4 ns^4
    A = zeros(nc, nc, nc, ns)
    for i to nc {
        for j to nc {
            for k to nc {
                for s to ns {
                    t = 0.0
                    for l to nc {
                        t = t + G[i, j, k, l] * M[l, s]
                    }
                    A[i, j, k, s] = t
                }
            }
        }
    }
    B = zeros(nc, nc, ns, ns)
    for i to nc {
        for j to nc {
            for r to ns {
                for s to ns {
                    t = 0.0
                    for k to nc {
                        t = t + A[i, j, k, s] * M[k, r]
                    }
                    B[i, j, r, s] = t
                }
            }
        }
    }
    C = zeros(nc, ns, ns, ns)
    for i to nc {
        for q to ns {
            for r to ns {
                for s to ns {
                    t = 0.0
                    for j to nc {
                        t = t + B[i, j, r, s] * M[j, q]
                    }
                    C[i, q, r, s] = t
                }
            }
        }
    }
    Y = zeros(ns, ns, ns, ns)
    for p to ns {
        for q to ns {
            for r to ns {
                for s to ns {
                    t = 0.0
                    for i to nc {
                        t = t + C[i, q, r, s] * M[i, p]
                    }
                    Y[p, q, r, s] = t
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return Y
}

#| Packed Cartesian ERIs to packed spherical ones through the dense view and
#| four quarter transformations. Meant for n <= 30, like `es_unpack8`.
def tr4_quarter(Gp: Arr, M: Arr, shells: Arr) -> Arr {
    nc = bas_nao_cart(shells)
    ns = bas_nao_sph(shells)
    G = es_unpack8(Gp, nc)
    Y = tr4_dense(G, M, nc, ns)
    return es_pack8(Y, ns)
}

#| The spherical block `[ma, mb, mc, md]` of shells (a, b, c, d), from the
#| Cartesian block read out of the packed vector and the four `M_l` blocks.
#| Any order of the four shells works, because `es_index8` accepts any order.
#| A shell up to p is the same in both sets, so its pass is skipped.
#@ df9ccdbc
def tr4_quartet(Gp: Arr, M: Arr, shells: Arr, a, b, c, d) -> Arr {
    ca = round(shells[a, SH_CART0])
    cb = round(shells[b, SH_CART0])
    cc = round(shells[c, SH_CART0])
    cd = round(shells[d, SH_CART0])
    sa = round(shells[a, SH_SPH0])
    sb = round(shells[b, SH_SPH0])
    sc = round(shells[c, SH_SPH0])
    sd = round(shells[d, SH_SPH0])
    na = round(shells[a, SH_NCART])
    nb = round(shells[b, SH_NCART])
    nk = round(shells[c, SH_NCART])
    nd = round(shells[d, SH_NCART])
    ma = round(shells[a, SH_NSPH])
    mb = round(shells[b, SH_NSPH])
    mc = round(shells[c, SH_NSPH])
    md = round(shells[d, SH_NSPH])
    g = zeros(na, nb, nk, nd)
    for i to na {
        for j to nb {
            for k to nk {
                for l to nd {
                    g[i, j, k, l] = Gp[es_index8(ca + i, cb + j, cc + k, cd + l)]
                }
            }
        }
    }
    # the last index first, then the third, second and first. A shell up to
    # p has the same functions in both sets (its block of M is the identity,
    # [test "a shell up to p is the same in both sets"]), so its pass is
    # skipped and the block goes on as it is.
    h = g
    if shells[d, SH_L] > 1 {
        h = zeros(na, nb, nk, md)
        for i to na {
            for j to nb {
                for k to nk {
                    for s to md {
                        t = 0.0
                        for l to nd {
                            t = t + g[i, j, k, l] * M[cd + l, sd + s]
                        }
                        h[i, j, k, s] = t
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    e = h
    if shells[c, SH_L] > 1 {
        e = zeros(na, nb, mc, md)
        for i to na {
            for j to nb {
                for r to mc {
                    for s to md {
                        t = 0.0
                        for k to nk {
                            t = t + h[i, j, k, s] * M[cc + k, sc + r]
                        }
                        e[i, j, r, s] = t
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    f = e
    if shells[b, SH_L] > 1 {
        f = zeros(na, mb, mc, md)
        for i to na {
            for q to mb {
                for r to mc {
                    for s to md {
                        t = 0.0
                        for j to nb {
                            t = t + e[i, j, r, s] * M[cb + j, sb + q]
                        }
                        f[i, q, r, s] = t
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    y = f
    if shells[a, SH_L] > 1 {
        y = zeros(ma, mb, mc, md)
        for p to ma {
            for q to mb {
                for r to mc {
                    for s to md {
                        t = 0.0
                        for i to na {
                            t = t + f[i, q, r, s] * M[ca + i, sa + p]
                        }
                        y[p, q, r, s] = t
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return y
}

#| Packed Cartesian ERIs to packed spherical ones, one canonical shell
#| quartet at a time: a >= b, c >= d and pair(a, b) >= pair(c, d). Every
#| canonical spherical (pq|rs) lies in one of those quartets, since the
#| symmetry that maps a function quartet to its canonical order maps its
#| shell quartet the same way.
#@ 1d51440b
def tr4_blocks(Gp: Arr, M: Arr, shells: Arr) -> Arr {
    ns = bas_nao_sph(shells)
    out = zeros(es_len8(ns))
    nsh = len(shells)
    #: canonical shell quartets only (a >= b, c >= d, cd <= ab)
    for a to nsh {
        for b to a + 1 {
            for c to a + 1 {
                for d to c + 1 {
                    if es_pair(c, d) <= es_pair(a, b) {
                        y = tr4_quartet(Gp, M, shells, a, b, c, d)
                        sa = round(shells[a, SH_SPH0])
                        sb = round(shells[b, SH_SPH0])
                        sc = round(shells[c, SH_SPH0])
                        sd = round(shells[d, SH_SPH0])
                        for p to round(shells[a, SH_NSPH]) {
                            for q to round(shells[b, SH_NSPH]) {
                                for r to round(shells[c, SH_NSPH]) {
                                    for s to round(shells[d, SH_NSPH]) {
                                        out[es_index8(sa + p, sb + q, sc + r, sd + s)] = y[p, q, r, s]
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return out
}

#| The ERIs over spherical functions in two passes: every Cartesian quartet
#| by `mderi_screened` (lib/hf/md_eri), then `tr4_blocks`. The production
#| build, `mderi_sph`, moves each quartet as it is computed; this is the path
#| it is held to, with the same signature, `(atoms, shells, prims, M)`.
#@ 0aa86860
def tr4_two_pass(atoms: Arr, shells: Arr, prims: Arr, M: Arr) -> Arr {
    return tr4_blocks(mderi_screened(atoms, shells, prims), M, shells)
}

# ---- test helpers ---------------------------------------------------------------

#| The per-quartet form computed for a shell quartet and for three of its
#| symmetric images, (b a | d c), (c d | a b) and (d c | b a); the largest
#| gap between matching entries. `tr4_blocks` computes only the canonical
#| order, so this checks that the order it picks does not matter.
def tr4_block_sym_defect(Gp: Arr, M: Arr, shells: Arr) {
    nsh = len(shells)
    w = 0.0
    for a to nsh {
        for b to nsh {
            for c to nsh {
                for d to nsh {
                    y = tr4_quartet(Gp, M, shells, a, b, c, d)
                    y1 = tr4_quartet(Gp, M, shells, b, a, d, c)
                    y2 = tr4_quartet(Gp, M, shells, c, d, a, b)
                    y3 = tr4_quartet(Gp, M, shells, d, c, b, a)
                    for p to round(shells[a, SH_NSPH]) {
                        for q to round(shells[b, SH_NSPH]) {
                            for r to round(shells[c, SH_NSPH]) {
                                for s to round(shells[d, SH_NSPH]) {
                                    v = y[p, q, r, s]
                                    w = max(w, abs(v - y1[q, p, s, r]))
                                    w = max(w, abs(v - y2[r, s, p, q]))
                                    w = max(w, abs(v - y3[s, r, q, p]))
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return w
}

